title Routing and dispatch — orchestrate.decide, router.route_request, both fallbacks and the hard "rag" default\n(evidence: orchestrator/app/engines/orchestrate.py:127-156 · orchestrator/app/engines/router.py:52-133 · orchestrator/app/graph.py:29-107 · orchestrator/app/main.py:403-643 · orchestrator/app/llm.py:78,112,283-299 · orchestrator/app/config.py:61,64,138,264)

participant "orchestrator · worker()\nmain.py:383-677" as orch #7C3AED
participant "engines/orchestrate\ndecide() · main.py:412" as decide #7C3AED
participant "graph · LangGraph\nget_graph().ainvoke() · graph.py:90-107" as graph #7C3AED
participant "engines/router\nroute_request() · router.py:92" as router #7C3AED

box "GPU — two independent vLLM endpoints, neither health-gated on this path" #FEF2F2
participant "vllm-router (AGENT)\nROUTER_BASE_URL · ROUTER_MODEL" as vrouter #16A34A
participant "vllm (MAIN)\nOPENAI_BASE_URL · MAIN_MODEL" as vmain #16A34A
end box

participant "graph engine nodes\n_sql · _rag · _vision · _report · _chat" as engines #7C3AED

autonumber

== A · Pre-flight: should this turn be an agent turn, or use web search? ==

orch -> decide : decide(text, history, effort) — gated by\ntext AND no pdf_data AND no image_data AND NOT request.agent\nmain.py:404-412
decide -> decide : allowances(effort) — ALLOWED ceiling\nfast: no/no · low: no/yes · medium: yes/yes · high: yes/yes\norchestrate.py:114-124

alt effort permits nothing, or the message is blank
  decide --> orch : Plan(agent=False, search=False) — short circuit, no model call\norchestrate.py:135-136
else a classification is worth making
  decide -> vrouter : HTTP POST /v1/chat/completions · JSON · async\ntemperature 0.0 · max_tokens 40 · enable_thinking: false\ninput clipped to ROUTER_INPUT_CHAR_CAP = 6000\norchestrate.py:138-140, llm.py:283-299
  vrouter --> decide : assistant text (expected to contain one JSON object)
  decide -> decide : parse_plan(raw) — accepts fields only on literal True\norchestrate.py:98-110
end

note over decide, vrouter #FEE2E2
  **F1 — this call sits ahead of the very first `status` frame on every text
  turn** (main.py:412 precedes :415) and has **no timeout of its own**. The
  only bound is the shared 300 s httpx client timeout (config.py:264 applied
  at llm.py:78). A wedged router endpoint therefore means 300 s of a
  completely silent UI before anything is emitted.
  **F9** — the extractor is a greedy `\{.*\}` (orchestrate.py:38) with no
  think-block and no code-fence stripping, unlike router.py and agent.py; two
  JSON objects in the output span from the first brace to the last and fail.
  Both parse failure and transport failure land in the same bare
  `except Exception: return Plan(False, False)` (orchestrate.py:142-144) —
  a dead GPU and a malformed answer are **indistinguishable**, and nothing
  is logged.
end note

decide --> orch : Plan(agent, search) — intersected with the effort ceiling (orchestrate.py:145-146);\neffort "high" AND search implies agent = True (orchestrate.py:154-155)
orch -> orch : emit("status", describe(plan)) — first SSE frame the user sees\nmain.py:415

== B · Engine dispatch — first match wins (main.py:551-643) ==

note over orch #FFFBEB
  The nine-branch chain in front of the graph, in source order:
  pdf → vision → repo → url → **agent** → search → dataset → assistant-chat,
  and only then the LangGraph fallback (main.py:551-643). The router below is
  reached **only** on the last branch: salesforce mode, no attachment, no
  agent, no repo/url/dataset follow-up.
end note

alt want_agent (explicit request.agent or auto_plan.agent)
  orch -> orch : run_agent_engine(...) — see 09-seq-agent-loop; the router is never consulted\nmain.py:592-601
else salesforce mode, no attachment — LangGraph fallback
  orch -> graph : ainvoke(message, session_id, image_base64, history, emit, model_choice, effort)\nin-process await · main.py:633-643
  graph -> router : _router_node → route_request(message, bool(image_base64), history)\ngraph.py:29-41

  alt an image is attached
    router --> graph : "vision" — short circuit **before any model call**\nrouter.py:103-104
  else short follow-up: 12 words or fewer
    router -> router : prepend the previous user turn, truncated to 400 chars,\nas "(earlier question: ...)" so the follow-up inherits its subject\nrouter.py:106-110
  end

  == B1 · Primary classification on the small model ==

  router -> vrouter : HTTP POST /v1/chat/completions · JSON · async\ntemperature 0.0 · max_tokens 200 · enable_thinking: false\nrouter.py:115-117, llm.py:283-299

  alt primary call and parse both succeed
    vrouter --> router : raw assistant text
    router -> router : parse_route(raw): strip think block → take the FIRST fenced body →\nslice first brace to last brace → json.loads → obj["route"] must be in\nROUTES = (sql, rag, vision, report, chat)\nrouter.py:52-80
    router --> graph : route
  else primary raised — connection refused, 400 window error or read timeout
    note over router #FEE2E2
      **Swallowed fallback #1** — `except Exception: pass` (router.py:121-122).
      All three causes are handled identically and **nothing is logged
      anywhere in this module**.
    end note

    == B2 · Retry on the MAIN model ==

    router -> vmain : HTTP POST /v1/chat/completions · JSON · async\ntemperature 0.0 · **max_tokens 50**\nrouter.py:126
    note over router, vmain #FEE2E2
      llm.chat_completion forces **enable_thinking: True** (llm.py:112), so a
      thinking preamble is expected here — and max_tokens 50 can be consumed
      **entirely by reasoning**, leaving parse_route nothing to match. This
      retry is far more likely to fail than the primary it is covering for.
    end note

    alt fallback call and parse both succeed
      vmain --> router : raw assistant text
      router --> graph : route
    else fallback also raised
      note over router #FEE2E2
        **Swallowed fallback #2** — a second bare `except Exception: pass`
        (router.py:130-131), again with no logging.
      end note
      router --> graph : **hard default "rag"** — unconditional return\nrouter.py:133
    end
  end

  note over router, vmain #FEE2E2
    **F8 — honest timeout accounting.** Neither call has a timeout of its own:
    `rg wait_for|timeout` over engines/*.py hits only url.py:36 and
    search.py:315/338. The single bound is LLM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT = 300 s
    (config.py:264 → llm.py:78). A wedged router endpoint **plus** a wedged
    main endpoint therefore costs **600 s** before "rag" is returned — and
    that 600 s is *on top of* the up-to-300 s already spent in step A.
    There is no retry beyond this one hop, no circuit breaker, and **no
    confidence score at all**: parse_route returns a route or None; no
    threshold, probability or logprob exists anywhere in the file.
    Consequence of the default: a total classifier outage silently sends
    greetings and small talk into the vector-search engine.
  end note

  note over router #FFFBEB
    **F2 — the think-strip is defeated on exactly the path where it matters.**
    parse_route's strict branch reads the stripped text `t` (router.py:66) but
    the lenient regex branch searches the **original, un-stripped** argument
    (router.py:77), so a route word mentioned inside a think block can win over
    the model's actual JSON answer.
  end note

  == B3 · Conditional edge to exactly five nodes ==

  graph -> engines : conditional_edges on state["route"], map = sql | rag | vision | report | chat\ngraph.py:100-104
  note over graph, engines #FFFBEB
    There is **no default branch** in the edge map — a route outside ROUTES
    would be a LangGraph KeyError. The only thing preventing it is
    parse_route's membership check (router.py:71-73) plus the "rag" default,
    i.e. correctness by construction rather than by guard. Every engine node
    then goes straight to END (graph.py:105-106).
    state["emit"] and state["message"] are read with bracket access
    (graph.py:47,54,62,70,80-81) — a missing key surfaces as the terminal
    `error` event. No timeouts, no retries in graph.py.
  end note
  engines -> engines : each node runs its engine and emits its OWN single final `meta` carrying `route`
  engines --> graph : {"answer": str}
  graph --> orch : state["answer"] · main.py:644
  note over orch #FFFBEB
    ChatState declares `route`, but main.py never reads it — the route the UI
    displays comes from the engine's own meta payload, not from graph state.
  end note
end

